  "enhanced_information": "A 22-year-old female at 24 weeks gestation presents with burning upon urination and a urine culture showing >100,000 CFU of E. coli, indicating a urinary tract infection (UTI). Given her history of a similar UTI 7 weeks ago, there is a risk of recurrent infections. In pregnancy, the choice of antibiotics must prioritize safety for both the mother and fetus. Nitrofurantoin and Cephalexin are both considered safe options for treating UTIs in pregnancy, while Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) is less preferred, especially in the third trimester due to potential risks. Prophylaxis may be warranted due to her recurrent UTIs. Therefore, the most appropriate management is Nitrofurantoin, with the option to continue prophylaxis for the remainder of the pregnancy, as it is effective and safe. This aligns with current medical consensus on UTI management in pregnant patients.",
